-- title:
--   Openness of the image of a formally étale uniformisation family
-- statement:
--   The setting is the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison at a prime $r$, stated for a fine moduli scheme of fake elliptic curves with full level structure.
--
--   **Base data.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, and a nonzero natural number $N$ divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$. A characteristic-zero commutative domain $\mathcal O$, assumed to be a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), together with an irreducible element $\pi$ such that $\mathcal O$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements, and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (absolute unramifiedness); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field realised as the fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Unramified extension.** A characteristic-zero commutative domain $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $F_r$, subject to: completeness of $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), maximality of that ideal (`hOnr_max`), integrality of every element over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`: each $x$ satisfies $p(x) \equiv 0 \bmod \pi$ for some monic $p \in \mathcal O[X]$), solvability modulo $\pi$ of every monic polynomial of positive degree over $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (`hOnr_closed`), and the congruence $F_r(x) \equiv x^{r} \bmod \pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`). Further, a monoid homomorphism $v_{\det} : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $v_{\det}(g) = n$ holds precisely when $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Indefinite side and coarse moduli.** Rationals $a, b$ with `hB`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a, b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra if and only if $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$. A $\mathbb Z$-submodule $\Lambda \subseteq \mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a, b]$ which is a maximal order (an order: contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans the algebra over $\mathbb Q$ and is finitely generated; and maximal among orders). A scheme $\mathcal X$ with structure morphism $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathcal O$ and an assignment `pt` sending each commutative ring $S$, each morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathcal O$ and each fake elliptic curve with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ datum over $S$ to a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} S \to \mathcal X$ over $s$; `h𝒳` asserts that these data form a coarse moduli space (`IsCoarseModuli`): invariance of `pt` under isomorphism, compatibility with base change along ring maps, bijectivity of `pt` on points with values in algebraically closed fields up to isomorphism of fake elliptic curves, and the universal property among all such point-assignments.
--
--   **Fine moduli at full level $n$.** A natural number $n$ with $3 \le n$, not divisible by $r$ or $\bar r$, and coprime to $N$; a scheme $M$ with $f_M : M \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and an assignment `ptF` on fake elliptic curves with full level-$n$ structure, such that `hM` holds (`IsFineModuli`): invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change, and surjectivity together with injectivity up to isomorphism of `ptF` on $S$-points for every commutative ring $S$. A group $G$, a homomorphism $\rho : G \to \operatorname{Aut} M$ and a labelling $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ with `hρ` (`IsLevelTwistAction`, six clauses): each $\rho(g)$ is a morphism over the base, twisting a full level structure by $\chi(g)$ translates `ptF` by $\rho(g)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, injective and surjective modulo $n\Lambda$ onto the classes invertible modulo $n$.
--
--   **Degeneracy and Atkin–Lehner data.** A morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ over the base ($p \circ f_M$-compatibility `hp`), invariant under $\rho$ (`hρp`), and compatible with the forgetful passage from full level $n$ to level $N$ on points (`hp_pt`). For each prime $\ell$ distinct from $r$ and $\bar r$: a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with point-assignment `ptT` on fake elliptic curves with an extra $\ell$-level subgroup, forming a coarse moduli space (`h𝒴`, `IsCoarseModuliT`, with the same five clauses in the extra-level setting); two morphisms $d_0, d_1 : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) with $d_0$ computing the underlying curve on points (`hd₀`) and $d_1$ computing the target of an $\ell$-level isogeny on points (`hd₁`). Two endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base which, on points, send a fake elliptic curve to its Atkin–Lehner quotient at $r$, respectively at $\bar r$ (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **Definite side.** Rationals $a_1, b_1$ with `hdef`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$, and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a_1, b_1] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly for $v$ above $\bar r$. Submodules $\Lambda_1, R_1$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a_1, b_1]$ with $\Lambda_1$ a maximal order, $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$), and $R_1 \le \Lambda_1$. An adelic unit $n_1$ lying in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ (integrality of $n_1$ and of $r\,n_1^{-1}$ for the adelic box of $R_1$, with $n_1^{-1}$ and $r^{-1} n_1$ not integral), such that $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is an Eichler order of level $N r$ (`hS₁`). An injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_0 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a_1, b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$, and a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ containing $r$. A subgroup $\Gamma^t$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a_1, b_1]^\times$ whose members are exactly the units lying, at every place $w \neq v$, in the subgroup generated by the local box units of $R_1$ (`hΓt`). Families $s_\ell$ of global units and $s^f_\ell$ of adelic units indexed by the primes $\ell \neq r, \bar r$, with `hs` (four clauses for each $\ell$): $s^f_\ell$ has component $s_\ell$ at every place not above $r$ and component $1$ at the places above $r$; the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $(s^f_\ell)^{-1}$ lies in the set `levelHeckeUSet` of $\Lambda_1$ and $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ at $\ell$ when $\ell \mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ at $\ell$ otherwise; and the reduced norm of $s_\ell$ equals $\ell$. The groups $\Gamma^t_\ell$ are prescribed by `hΓtℓ` to be $\Gamma^t \cap s_\ell \Gamma^t s_\ell^{-1}$. Finally a unit $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma^t$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   **The uniformisation family.** For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map
--   $$\Theta_f : \bigl( \operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal O\text{-alg}}(\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}, B) \times \mathrm{DeligneDatum}_{K_0,\pi}(B) \bigr) \times G \longrightarrow \{\varphi : \operatorname{Spec} B \to M \mid \varphi \text{ over } \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O\},$$
--   the target being the value at $B$ of `Scheme.nilpPoints fM`. The hypothesis `hnat` states that $\Theta_f$ is natural: for every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\varphi : B \to B'$ between such algebras, $\Theta_f$ commutes with the induced maps on source and target. The hypothesis `het` is the formal étaleness clause: for every surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map $p : B \to B_0$ between $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebras whose kernel is square-zero (any two elements killed by $p$ have product zero), and for every $x_0$ in the source functor at $B_0$ and every $y$ in `Scheme.nilpPoints fM` at $B$ with $p_*y = \Theta_f(x_0)$, there is a unique $x$ at $B$ with $p_*x = x_0$ and $\Theta_f(x) = y$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exists a subset $V$ of the underlying space of $M$ such that $V$ is open, and such that for every point $x$ of $M$ the following two conditions are equivalent.
--
--   First: $x \in V$, and there exist a type $k$ carrying a field structure and making it algebraically closed, an $\mathcal O$-algebra structure on $k$ for which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, and an element $y$ of `Scheme.nilpPoints fM` at $k$ — that is, a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} k \to M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ — whose underlying map of spaces sends the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} k$ to $x$.
--
--   Second: there exist such $k$, such an $\mathcal O$-algebra structure with $\pi$ nilpotent, and such a $y$ centred at $x$, and in addition an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\psi : \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to k$, a Deligne datum $P$ over $k$ for $K_0$ and $\pi$, and an element $g$ of $G$ with $\Theta_f(((\psi, P), g)) = y$.
--
--   Thus the trace of the open set $V$ on those points of $M$ which support a geometric point with $\pi$ mapping to zero is exactly the set of points covered by the family $\Theta_f$ on algebraically closed $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebras.
--
--   This is the openness step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison: the locus of the special fibre of the fine moduli scheme of fake elliptic curves with full level-$n$ structure that is reached by the formally étale uniformisation family $\Theta_f$ built from $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$, Deligne's functor $\hat\Omega$ and the twisting group $G$ is cut out by an open subset of $M$. It feeds the subsequent statement that every point of the special fibre is covered by the uniformisation, and it relies on the local openness statement for a formally étale family over a base change of $M$ together with the fact that a fine moduli scheme of this kind is locally of finite presentation over $\mathcal O$.
